XARGS(1)                       P32W Unix                        XARGS(1)

NAME
       xargs - build and run commands from the standard input

SYNOPSIS
       xargs [-0rtx] [-E eof] [-I repl | -L n | -n n] [-s size]
             [command [arg ...]]

DESCRIPTION
       xargs reads words from its standard input and runs command
       (echo if none is given), with its args, followed by as many
       of the words as fit; it runs it again for the next words,
       until the input ends. If the input has no words command is
       run once with no more arguments (but see -r).

       Words are separated by blanks and newlines. In them '...' and
       "..." quote everything up to the closing quote (which must be
       on the same line), and \ quotes the next character, blank or
       newline included; so '' is an empty word.

       A P32W Unix program may be given at most 249 arguments,
       counting its name, and 4096 bytes of arguments and
       environment strings, each with its NUL. xargs keeps each
       command within these limits; -s may set a smaller one.

       command is looked for in each directory of $PATH as by sh.
       Its standard input is /dev/null; xargs waits for each run to
       finish before the next.

OPTIONS
       -0     words end at NUL bytes; quotes, backslashes, blanks
              and newlines are ordinary characters
       -E eof the word eof is the end of the input
       -I repl
              run command once for each line of input (less its
              leading blanks; blanks in it do not separate words,
              but quotes and \ work as above), with every repl in
              command and its args replaced by the line; empty lines
              are skipped
       -L n   run command with the words of n lines at a time (a
              line ending in a blank goes on to the next one)
       -n n   run command with at most n words at a time
       -r     do not run command if the input has no words
       -s size
              each command line (each string and its NUL) is at
              most size bytes; the largest size is 4096 less the
              size of the environment, and a larger one is reduced
              to it
       -t     write each command line to the standard error before
              it is run
       -x     with -n or -L: stop (status 1) if a command line
              would be too long to hold the n words or lines

       -I, -L and -n override each other: the last given counts.

EXAMPLES
       find . -name '*.o' | xargs rm
       ls | xargs -n 1 wc            one wc for each file
       ls *.c | xargs -I X cp X /tmp/X.save
       cat list | xargs -t -n 2 cmp

EXIT STATUS
       0      every run of command exited 0
       123    a run exited with status 1-254 (xargs goes on)
       124    a run exited with status 255 (xargs stops)
       125    a run was killed by a signal (xargs stops)
       126    command was found but could not be run
       127    command was not found
       1      a usage error, a quote not closed, or a word (or
              command line) that cannot fit

SEE ALSO
       find(1), sh(1), echo(1)

BUGS
       There is no -p (prompt) and no -P (run in parallel). A
       program that is found but then fails to start (not an a.out)
       gives status 123, not 126.
